{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>Who is like You among the elim, YHWH</b> — among the mighty, as in \"and he took the mighty ones of the land\" (Ezekiel 17:13), \"my strength, hasten to my help\" (Psalms 22:20). This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Exodus 15:11). And in truth the expression does mean strength and might, but <b>who is like You among the elim</b> — among the angels on high, who are called elim, from the expression \"this is my El and I will glorify Him\" (Exodus 15:2), and the Holy One, blessed be He, is called El Elyon above them all. And so \"he shall speak wondrous things against the El of elim\" (Daniel 11:36), like \"He is the Elohim of the elohim\" (Deuteronomy 10:17), and so \"ascribe to YHWH, O sons of elim\" (Psalms 29:1), like \"sons of the Elohim,\" for they are called elim and sons of elim, and likewise they are called elohim and sons of elohim: \"for YHWH is greater than all the elohim\" (Exodus 18:11), \"and the sons of the Elohim came to present themselves before YHWH\" (Job 1:6). And there are some who say (Radak in Sefer HaMikhlol 2) that \"sons of\" is not a construct noun, and so \"sons of the third rank\" (Genesis 50:23), \"men portrayed on the wall\" (Ezekiel 23:14). And this is the meaning of <b>Who is like You, majestic in holiness</b> — that none is like Him, mighty in the abode of holiness in the heavens. And in the Mekhilta (here): Who is like You among these who serve before You on high, as it is said (Psalms 89:7–8): \"For who in the heavens can be compared to YHWH, who among the sons of elim can be likened to YHWH, an El dreaded in the great council of the holy ones.\"" "<b>Awesome in praises</b> — He is feared from having His praises declared, lest they be diminished, as in \"to You silence is praise\" (Psalms 65:2). This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Exodus 15:11). And R. Abraham ibn Ezra also explained that all who praise Him are afraid to praise His name, for who can express all His praise, yet they are obligated to praise His name, for He alone <b>does wonders</b>. In my opinion, the meaning of <b>awesome in praises</b> is that He is awesome through praises, for He does awesome things and is praised for them — He took vengeance upon those who transgress His will, and through them saved His servants, and behold in this He is awesome and exceedingly praised. And because the kings of the earth are awesome through oppression and wrongdoing, it says that YHWH is awesome through the things for which He is praised. And likewise \"to You silence is praise\" according to the plain sense means: the hope directed to You is praise, for all who hope to You obtain their desire and praise Your name in Zion and there fulfill the vow they made in their time of distress — from the expression \"my soul, be silent to Elohim, for from Him is my hope\" (Psalms 62:6), \"and the sun was silent, and the moon stood still\" (Joshua 10:13). And so \"surely to Elohim my soul is silent\" — hoping. So it appears to me. And others I have heard say: to You praise falls silent, for no praise can grasp Your great and awesome deeds, from the expression \"and night, and no silence is to me\" (Psalms 22:3), and the meaning of \"Elohim in Zion\" (Psalms 65:2) would be: the Elohim who is in Zion — that is, who dwells there. But what we said is the correct interpretation."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {0 "Radak's citation reads 'בספר המכלול י ב' — 'in Sefer HaMikhlol, part 2'; I have rendered the Hebrew numeral directly."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:08:53.368225699Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}